WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:00.450 --> 00:07.770
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

00:11.010 --> 00:16.890
Good afternoon and welcome.

00:16.890 --> 00:21.260
In my opening remarks today, I'll briefly review today's policy decision.

00:21.260 --> 00:23.140
And I'll place the decision in the context

00:23.140 --> 00:26.380
of our economic projections and our policy strategy.

00:26.380 --> 00:28.870
I'll then be glad to take your questions.

00:28.870 --> 00:31.720
Throughout today's briefing, my goal will be to reflect the consensus

00:31.720 --> 00:36.330
of the Committee while taking note of the diversity of views, as appropriate.

00:36.330 --> 00:40.740
Of course, my remarks and interpretations are my own responsibility.

00:40.740 --> 00:44.120
As indicated in the policy statement released earlier this afternoon,

00:44.120 --> 00:46.230
the Committee decided today to keep the target range

00:46.230 --> 00:49.650
for the federal funds rate at 0 to ¼ percent.

00:49.650 --> 00:52.190
The Committee continues to anticipate

00:52.190 --> 00:55.940
that economic conditions-including low rates of resource utilization

00:55.940 --> 00:59.440
and a subdued outlook for inflation in the medium run-are likely

00:59.440 --> 01:01.050
to warrant exceptionally low levels

01:01.050 --> 01:04.670
for the federal funds rate for an extended period.

01:04.670 --> 01:07.150
The Committee's planned purchases of $600 billion

01:07.150 --> 01:11.440
of longer-term Treasury securities will be completed by the end of this month,

01:11.440 --> 01:13.990
and the Committee will continue to reinvest principal payments

01:13.990 --> 01:16.540
from its securities holdings going forward.

01:16.540 --> 01:21.970
In conjunction with today's meeting, the FOMC participants submitted projections

01:21.970 --> 01:24.560
for economic growth, the unemployment rate,

01:24.560 --> 01:30.700
and the inflation rate for the years 2011 to 2013 and over the longer run.

01:30.700 --> 01:34.230
These projections are conditional on each participant's individual assessment

01:34.230 --> 01:37.030
of the appropriate path of monetary policy needed

01:37.030 --> 01:40.140
to best promote the Committee's objectives.

01:40.140 --> 01:43.000
I'll focus on the information shown in the figures that have been distributed.

01:43.000 --> 01:48.000
In each figure, the dark area denotes the central tendencies

01:48.000 --> 01:52.130
of our current projections, while the lighter shaded area denotes the full range

01:52.130 --> 01:54.020
of projections.

01:54.020 --> 01:56.610
The longer-run projections, shown at the right of each figure,

01:56.610 --> 01:59.290
represent participants' assessments of the rate

01:59.290 --> 02:03.400
to which each variable will converge over time under appropriate monetary policy

02:03.400 --> 02:06.270
and assuming no further shocks to the economy.

02:06.270 --> 02:12.000
The longer-run projections for output growth have a central tendency of 2.5

02:12.000 --> 02:14.960
to 2.8 percent, and the longer-run projections

02:14.960 --> 02:19.920
for the unemployment rate have a central tendency of 5.2 to 5.6 percent-the same

02:19.920 --> 02:22.340
as in our April projections.

02:22.340 --> 02:23.930
These projections may be interpreted

02:23.930 --> 02:28.060
as indicating participants' current estimates of the economy's normal

02:28.060 --> 02:31.140
or trend rate of growth and its normal unemployment rate

02:31.140 --> 02:33.350
over the longer run respectively.

02:33.350 --> 02:37.320
It should be noted that these estimates are inherently uncertain

02:37.320 --> 02:41.150
and subject to revision, because longer-run rates of economic growth

02:41.150 --> 02:45.350
and unemployment are determined largely by nonmonetary factors that may evolve

02:45.350 --> 02:49.650
over time and that often cannot be directly measured.

02:49.650 --> 02:53.300
The central tendency of the longer-run projections for inflation,

02:53.300 --> 02:54.590
as measured by the price index

02:54.590 --> 02:59.870
for personal consumption expenditures, is 1.7 to 2.0 percent.

02:59.870 --> 03:02.760
Since the longer-run inflation outlook is determined almost entirely

03:02.760 --> 03:06.160
by monetary policy, these projections can be interpreted

03:06.160 --> 03:10.440
as indicating the inflation rate that each of the participants judge

03:10.440 --> 03:13.060
to be consistent with the Federal Reserve's mandate

03:13.060 --> 03:16.620
of fostering maximum employment and stable prices.

03:16.620 --> 03:19.730
In effect, the "mandate consistent" inflation rate is judged

03:19.730 --> 03:22.090
to be 2 percent or a bit less.

03:22.090 --> 03:26.770
I now turn to the contours of the Committee's economic outlook.

03:26.770 --> 03:29.260
As indicated in today's policy statement,

03:29.260 --> 03:32.770
the economic recovery appears to be proceeding at a moderate pace,

03:32.770 --> 03:35.680
though somewhat more slowly than the Committee had expected,

03:35.680 --> 03:40.070
and some recent labor market indicators have also been weaker than expected.

03:40.070 --> 03:44.710
For example, the unemployment rate has risen by 0.3 percentage points

03:44.710 --> 03:47.930
since March, and new claims for unemployment insurance have moved

03:47.930 --> 03:50.230
somewhat higher.

03:50.230 --> 03:53.810
The reduced pace of the recovery partly reflects factors

03:53.810 --> 03:56.140
that are likely to be temporary.

03:56.140 --> 03:59.490
In particular, consumers' purchasing power has been damped by higher food

03:59.490 --> 04:02.850
and energy prices; and the aftermath of the tragic earthquake

04:02.850 --> 04:06.260
and tsunami in Japan has been associated with disruptions

04:06.260 --> 04:10.010
in global supply chains, especially in the auto sector.

04:10.010 --> 04:13.780
However, some moderation in gasoline prices is now in prospect,

04:13.780 --> 04:17.050
and the effects of the Japanese disaster on manufacturing output are likely

04:17.050 --> 04:20.130
to dissipate in coming months.

04:20.130 --> 04:25.310
Consequently, as shown in the first figure, entitled "Change in Real GDP,"

04:25.310 --> 04:26.730
the Committee expects that the pace

04:26.730 --> 04:30.350
of economic recovery will pick up over coming quarters.

04:30.350 --> 04:31.980
Specifically, participants' projections

04:31.980 --> 04:37.430
for output growth have a central tendency of about 2.7 to 2.9 percent

04:37.430 --> 04:42.780
for this year and 3.3 to 3.7 percent for next year-growth rates faster

04:42.780 --> 04:45.600
than we have seen so far in 2011.

04:45.600 --> 04:48.240
However, Committee participants have also generally responded

04:48.240 --> 04:53.210
to the recent slowing by marking down the growth projections for 2011 and 2012,

04:53.210 --> 04:57.940
which are nearly a half percentage point lower than our April projections.

04:57.940 --> 05:00.640
Looking further ahead, the central tendency of the growth projections

05:00.640 --> 05:04.000
for 2012-2013, sorry-is 3.5 to 4.2 percent,

05:04.000 --> 05:07.750
essentially the same as in the April projections.

05:07.750 --> 05:10.250
As shown in the second figure, entitled "Unemployment Rate,"

05:10.250 --> 05:14.150
the unemployment rate is expected to resume its gradual decline towards levels

05:14.150 --> 05:18.210
that the Committee judges to be consistent with this dual mandate.

05:18.210 --> 05:20.530
In particular, the unemployment rate is projected to edge

05:20.530 --> 05:25.730
down over coming months to 8.6 to 8.9 percent in the fourth quarter of this year

05:25.730 --> 05:30.280
and then decline gradually over the subsequent two years to a level of 7.0

05:30.280 --> 05:34.270
to 7.5 percent in the fourth quarter of 2013,

05:34.270 --> 05:36.100
still well above the central tendency

05:36.100 --> 05:39.930
of participants' longer-run unemployment projections.

05:39.930 --> 05:43.450
In short, we expect the unemployment rate to continue to decline,

05:43.450 --> 05:47.930
but the pace of progress remains frustratingly slow.

05:47.930 --> 05:51.300
Inflation has moved up in recent months, mainly reflecting higher prices

05:51.300 --> 05:53.890
for some commodities and imported goods.

05:53.890 --> 05:57.280
In addition, prices of motor vehicles have risen notably as a result

05:57.280 --> 06:00.000
of the recent supply chain disruptions.

06:00.000 --> 06:03.010
However, as the effects of these factors dissipate,

06:03.010 --> 06:06.500
the Committee anticipates that inflation will subside in coming quarters

06:06.500 --> 06:09.460
to levels at or below its mandate-consistent rate,

06:09.460 --> 06:13.210
as shown in the figure entitled "PCE Inflation."

06:13.210 --> 06:17.470
Specifically, the central tendency of participants' inflation projections is 2.3

06:17.470 --> 06:23.790
to 2.5 percent for this year but declines to 1.5 to 2.0 percent in both 2012

06:23.790 --> 06:30.350
and 2013-a trajectory that is broadly similar to that of our April projections.

06:30.350 --> 06:34.790
The economic outlook provides important policy context.

06:34.790 --> 06:37.300
In particular, the Committee's policy strategy is intended

06:37.300 --> 06:40.450
to foster both aspects of our dual mandate-that is,

06:40.450 --> 06:44.200
promoting the economic recovery so that the unemployment rate returns over time

06:44.200 --> 06:48.320
to its longer-term normal level, and ensuring that inflation, over time,

06:48.320 --> 06:51.160
is at levels consistent with our mandate.

06:51.160 --> 06:55.030
At 9.1 percent, the current unemployment rate remains elevated,

06:55.030 --> 06:56.620
and progress towards more normal levels

06:56.620 --> 07:00.520
of unemployment is likely to be slow, as I noted.

07:00.520 --> 07:02.830
Moreover, the inflation rate, which picked up in recent months,

07:02.830 --> 07:07.200
is expected to subside to levels at or below the rate of 2 percent,

07:07.200 --> 07:09.210
or a bit less, that most participants judge

07:09.210 --> 07:12.450
to be most consistent with the dual mandate.

07:12.450 --> 07:16.690
The ongoing labor market slack and the subdued inflation outlook are key reasons

07:16.690 --> 07:19.800
for the Committee's decision to maintain the current high degree

07:19.800 --> 07:22.800
of monetary policy accommodation and for our judgment

07:22.800 --> 07:25.310
that exceptionally low levels of the funds rate are likely

07:25.310 --> 07:27.590
to be warranted for an extended period.

07:27.590 --> 07:28.090
Thank you.

07:28.090 --> 07:29.370
I'd be glad to take your questions.

07:29.370 --> 07:31.070
JON HILSENRATH.

07:31.070 --> 07:33.700
Jon Hilsenrath from the Wall Street Journal.

07:33.700 --> 07:38.320
Mr. Chairman, the FOMC says that it will maintain short-term interest rates

07:38.320 --> 07:41.560
at an exceptionally low level for an extended period.

07:41.560 --> 07:44.790
Does that policy-or, does that guidance also apply

07:44.790 --> 07:46.920
for the Fed's securities holdings?

07:46.920 --> 07:48.910
In other words, will they be maintained

07:48.910 --> 07:51.090
at a very high level for an extended period?

07:51.090 --> 07:53.130
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

07:53.130 --> 07:56.280
We haven't made any such commitment.

07:56.280 --> 08:03.850
It's true that when we begin to allow the portfolio to run off rather

08:03.850 --> 08:09.070
than reinvesting, that would be a first step in a process of exiting

08:09.070 --> 08:12.180
from our currently highly accommodative policies.

08:12.180 --> 08:16.850
But we've not yet chosen to make any particular commitment about the time frame.

08:16.850 --> 08:19.350
But we'll be looking at the outlook and trying to assess

08:19.350 --> 08:21.880
when the appropriate time is to take that step.

08:21.880 --> 08:22.960
JON HILSENRATH.

08:22.960 --> 08:25.180
If I may follow up, why give guidance on-sorry.

08:25.180 --> 08:28.010
If I may follow up, why give guidance on one policy tool but not give guidance

08:28.010 --> 08:31.320
on another policy tool when the Fed has talked

08:31.320 --> 08:33.070
about those two policy tools working together?

08:33.070 --> 08:34.170
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

08:34.170 --> 08:35.200
No, that's a good question.

08:35.200 --> 08:39.460
It's something we have on the table, something we've thought about.

08:39.460 --> 08:44.530
But to this point, we haven't taken that step.

08:44.530 --> 08:49.920
GREG IP. Mr. Chairman, the Committee lowered not just this year's central

08:49.920 --> 08:52.790
tendency forecast but also 2012.

08:52.790 --> 08:54.940
And yet, the statement of the Committee attributes most

08:54.940 --> 08:57.850
of the revision forecast to temporary factors.

08:57.850 --> 09:01.140
So I was wondering if you could explain what seems to be persisting in terms

09:01.140 --> 09:02.260
of holding the recovery back.

09:02.260 --> 09:04.390
I did see the statement says,

09:04.390 --> 09:06.520
"in part" to factors that are likely to be temporary.

09:06.520 --> 09:08.890
Are there more permanent factors

09:08.890 --> 09:12.820
that are producing a worse outlook than three months ago?

09:12.820 --> 09:13.720
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

09:13.720 --> 09:19.330
Well, as you-as you point out, what we say is that the temporary factors are

09:19.330 --> 09:21.830
"in part" the reason for the slowdown.

09:21.830 --> 09:24.330
In other words, part of the slowdown is temporary,

09:24.330 --> 09:26.760
and part of it may be longer lasting.

09:26.760 --> 09:32.810
We do believe that growth is going to pick up going into 2012

09:32.810 --> 09:37.770
but at a somewhat slower pace from-than we had anticipated in April.

09:37.770 --> 09:47.980
We don't have a precise read on why this slower pace of growth is persisting.

09:47.980 --> 09:52.740
One way to think about it is that maybe some of the headwinds

09:52.740 --> 10:00.440
that have been concerning us, like, you know, weakness in the financial sector,

10:00.440 --> 10:05.850
problems in the housing sector, balance sheet and deleveraging issues-some

10:05.850 --> 10:09.150
of these headwinds may be stronger or more persistent than we thought.

10:09.150 --> 10:15.820
And I think it's an appropriate balance to attribute a slowdown partly

10:15.820 --> 10:20.500
to these identifiable temporary factors but to acknowledge a possibility

10:20.500 --> 10:26.500
that some of the slowdown is due to factors which are longer lived

10:26.500 --> 10:29.450
and which will be still operative by next year.

10:29.450 --> 10:33.990
You note that, in 2013, we have growth at about the same rate

10:33.990 --> 10:36.590
that we anticipated in April.

10:36.590 --> 10:38.680
STEVE LIESMAN.

10:38.680 --> 10:43.040
Mr. Chairman, could you describe to what extent the situation in Greece

10:43.040 --> 10:45.510
and Europe was discussed at the meeting

10:45.510 --> 10:48.070
and what policy conclusions were reached?

10:48.070 --> 10:53.140
And also could you tell us whether or not, in response to the recent slowdown,

10:53.140 --> 10:55.670
there was a discussion about further easing?

10:55.670 --> 10:56.490
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

10:56.490 --> 11:00.840
Well, with respect to Greece, that's obviously very important.

11:00.840 --> 11:04.420
That-it's a very difficult situation.

11:04.420 --> 11:08.310
We've been in close communication with our colleagues

11:08.310 --> 11:11.060
in Europe-obviously not part of the negotiations,

11:11.060 --> 11:12.800
but we've been kept well informed.

11:12.800 --> 11:16.910
We had a G-7 call over the weekend, for example.

11:16.910 --> 11:24.480
I think the Europeans appreciate the incredible importance

11:24.480 --> 11:28.470
of resolving the Greek situation.

11:28.470 --> 11:31.850
If there were a failure to resolve that situation,

11:31.850 --> 11:34.820
it would pose threats to the European financial system,

11:34.820 --> 11:38.380
the global financial system, and to European political unity,

11:38.380 --> 11:40.170
I would conjecture as well.

11:40.170 --> 11:41.600
So, yes, we did discuss it.

11:41.600 --> 11:49.940
It's one of several potential financial risks that we're facing now.

11:49.940 --> 11:56.910
But, again, we are mostly just following the-the situation closely

11:56.910 --> 11:59.600
and making sure that, as best we can,

11:59.600 --> 12:06.090
that our own institutions are well positioned relative to sovereign debt

12:06.090 --> 12:08.850
in the so-called peripheral countries.

12:08.850 --> 12:12.890
With respect to additional asset purchases,

12:12.890 --> 12:18.190
we haven't taken any action, obviously, today.

12:18.190 --> 12:20.060
We'll be reviewing the outlook going forward.

12:20.060 --> 12:21.840
It will be a Committee decision.

12:21.840 --> 12:23.770
I think the point I would make, though,

12:23.770 --> 12:27.100
in terms of where we are today versus where we were, say,

12:27.100 --> 12:31.920
in August of last year when I began to talk about asset purchases,

12:31.920 --> 12:37.430
is that at that time, inflation was very low and falling.

12:37.430 --> 12:44.250
Many objective indicators suggested that deflation was a nontrivial risk.

12:44.250 --> 12:48.850
And I think that the securities purchases have been very successful

12:48.850 --> 12:50.780
in eliminating deflation risk.

12:50.780 --> 12:52.970
I don't think people appreciate necessarily

12:52.970 --> 12:57.270
that deflation can be a very pernicious situation

12:57.270 --> 13:02.430
where it could have very long-lasting effects on economic growth.

13:02.430 --> 13:09.000
In addition, growth in payrolls has actually picked up.

13:09.000 --> 13:14.660
In the four months before the Jackson Hole speech in August,

13:14.660 --> 13:18.350
there was about an 80,000 per month payroll increase.

13:18.350 --> 13:22.830
So far in 2011, including the weak payroll report in May,

13:22.830 --> 13:25.330
the average is closer to 180,000.

13:25.330 --> 13:27.740
So there has been improvement in the labor market,

13:27.740 --> 13:30.320
albeit not as strong as we would like.

13:30.320 --> 13:35.910
As of last August, we were essentially missing significantly

13:35.910 --> 13:38.680
in both-on both sides of our mandate.

13:38.680 --> 13:41.620
Inflation was too low and falling,

13:41.620 --> 13:45.960
and unemployment looked like it might be even beginning to rise again.

13:45.960 --> 13:51.840
In that case, the case for monetary action was pretty clear in my mind.

13:51.840 --> 13:55.470
I think we are in a different position today-certainly not where we'd

13:55.470 --> 14:02.580
like to be, but closer to the dual-mandate objectives than we were at that time.

14:02.580 --> 14:07.150
So, again, the situation is different today than last August,

14:07.150 --> 14:11.680
but we'll continue to monitor the economy and act as needed.

14:11.680 --> 14:13.680
LUCA DILEO.

14:13.680 --> 14:15.320
Luca DiLeo, Dow Jones.

14:15.320 --> 14:19.120
Would budget cuts, which may begin at the end of this year,

14:19.120 --> 14:21.120
help or hinder the economy?

14:21.120 --> 14:22.520
This goes back to the other question:

14:22.520 --> 14:26.690
What's fiscal policy like in the 2012 growth forecast?

14:26.690 --> 14:28.310
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

14:28.310 --> 14:35.830
The effect of fiscal cuts on the economy depends very much on the timing.

14:35.830 --> 14:42.160
I've advocated that the negotiations about the budget focus on the longer term,

14:42.160 --> 14:46.480
say, 10 years, which is the budget window, or even longer if you're taking

14:46.480 --> 14:50.590
into account entitlement reform, for example.

14:50.590 --> 14:56.480
By taking a long-run perspective, we can help the economy by reducing the risk

14:56.480 --> 14:59.450
that interest rates might rise suddenly.

14:59.450 --> 15:03.380
We may help increase confidence in the part of households and businesses.

15:03.380 --> 15:06.470
And so I think it's very desirable that we take strong action

15:06.470 --> 15:11.690
to lower our budget deficits over the longer term.

15:11.690 --> 15:16.310
In doing that, I think it would be best not to-in light of the weakness

15:16.310 --> 15:20.620
of the recovery, it would be best not to have sudden

15:20.620 --> 15:24.820
and sharp fiscal consolidation in the very near term.

15:24.820 --> 15:28.250
That doesn't do so much for the long-run budget situation.

15:28.250 --> 15:31.230
It just is a negative for growth.

15:31.230 --> 15:34.880
So my answer is that it depends very much on the timing,

15:34.880 --> 15:40.500
and I hope that the congressional negotiators will take a longer-term view

15:40.500 --> 15:42.660
as they-as they discuss the issue.

15:42.660 --> 15:45.060
DARREN GERSH.

15:45.060 --> 15:47.280
Darren Gersh with Nightly Business Report on PBS.

15:47.280 --> 15:50.450
If I could follow up on what he just said, there seems to be a growing view

15:50.450 --> 15:54.860
in the country that the deficit is the problem with jobs,

15:54.860 --> 15:57.840
and that immediate cuts in the deficit would grow the economy

15:57.840 --> 15:59.510
and immediately create jobs.

15:59.510 --> 16:01.650
Many economists disagree with that.

16:01.650 --> 16:04.880
Do you want to go a little further and maybe talk about that issue,

16:04.880 --> 16:07.980
and whether you agree with that view that seems to be taking root?

16:07.980 --> 16:08.500
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

16:08.500 --> 16:14.930
I don't think that sharp, immediate cuts in the deficit would create more jobs.

16:14.930 --> 16:20.130
I think in the very short run that we are seeing already a certain amount

16:20.130 --> 16:25.460
of fiscal drag coming from state and local governments,

16:25.460 --> 16:30.480
as well as from the withdrawal of previous federal stimulus.

16:30.480 --> 16:37.660
So I think in the very short run that, you know, the fiscal tightening is-is,

16:37.660 --> 16:42.950
at best, neutral but probably somewhat negative for job creation.

16:42.950 --> 16:46.810
I think what people will understand, should understand,

16:46.810 --> 16:53.800
is that our budgetary problems are very long run in nature.

16:53.800 --> 16:56.150
The projections made by the CBO, for example,

16:56.150 --> 17:03.570
talk about where our debt-to-GDP ratio will be in 2020, 2025, and so on.

17:03.570 --> 17:05.380
That doesn't mean we should wait to act.

17:05.380 --> 17:07.620
The sooner we can act, the better.

17:07.620 --> 17:13.230
But the most efficient and effective way to address our fiscal problems-and,

17:13.230 --> 17:17.040
again, I think this is extremely important-is to take a longer-run perspective,

17:17.040 --> 17:20.520
not to focus the cuts heavily on the near term.

17:20.520 --> 17:24.820
But by taking a long-run perspective and making a credible plan

17:24.820 --> 17:29.500
for reducing future deficits, we'll lower interest rates,

17:29.500 --> 17:35.140
or at least prevent them from rising, and we will increase confidence.

17:35.140 --> 17:37.210
And that could be very constructive.

17:37.210 --> 17:40.000
But if it's entirely focused on the near term,

17:40.000 --> 17:43.590
I don't think that's the optimal way to proceed.

17:43.590 --> 17:45.780
NEIL IRWIN.

17:45.780 --> 17:48.900
Mr. Chairman, Neil Irwin with the Washington Post.

17:48.900 --> 17:52.820
Do you believe that the FOMC has the authority to set a, say,

17:52.820 --> 17:56.520
2 percent inflation target on its own, unilaterally,

17:56.520 --> 17:58.700
or do you believe that it would need to go to Congress to get

17:58.700 --> 18:00.050
that target made more explicit?

18:00.050 --> 18:02.520
If the former, are you considering doing it?

18:02.520 --> 18:05.430
If the latter, are you considering going to Congress to ask them to do it?

18:05.430 --> 18:06.150
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

18:06.150 --> 18:12.490
Well, Neil, as you know, I've been a long-time proponent of an inflation target.

18:12.490 --> 18:16.510
I think that it would help anchor inflation expectations;

18:16.510 --> 18:19.120
it would make it easier to reach our inflation objective.

18:19.120 --> 18:23.160
At the same time, it's not at all inconsistent with our employment objective

18:23.160 --> 18:26.720
because keeping inflation low and stable, keeping inflation expectations low

18:26.720 --> 18:30.410
and stable, actually gives the Fed more leeway to respond

18:30.410 --> 18:33.130
to short-term shocks to the economy.

18:33.130 --> 18:36.870
So I think it's something, you know, that is worth considering.

18:36.870 --> 18:41.670
In terms of authorities, I would just say

18:41.670 --> 18:46.300
that there are multiple models around the world.

18:46.300 --> 18:48.860
So, for example, in the European Central Bank,

18:48.860 --> 18:54.590
that bank has a mandate for price stability, period.

18:54.590 --> 18:59.810
And they set their own definition of that using input

18:59.810 --> 19:03.040
from economists and others.

19:03.040 --> 19:09.620
So I don't think there's a real barrier to setting a target.

19:09.620 --> 19:14.490
However, it is very important that, first,

19:14.490 --> 19:18.440
that we communicate to the public what we're doing.

19:18.440 --> 19:21.400
Without sufficient explanation and background,

19:21.400 --> 19:25.260
many people might think that we were somehow abandoning our employment target.

19:25.260 --> 19:28.940
So we need to make sure that that's well understood both by the public

19:28.940 --> 19:32.960
and by Congress that having a target would not mean

19:32.960 --> 19:35.970
that we were abandoning the other leg of the dual mandate.

19:35.970 --> 19:38.620
Also, you asked about consulting with Congress.

19:38.620 --> 19:40.420
I think under any circumstances,

19:40.420 --> 19:43.490
it would be important to take the pulse of Congress.

19:43.490 --> 19:45.600
They-we might have the legal authority to do this,

19:45.600 --> 19:48.260
but I think we do need some buy-in from the Administration

19:48.260 --> 19:50.500
and Congress to take that step.

19:50.500 --> 19:55.130
We are continuing to periodically discuss this issue.

19:55.130 --> 19:59.410
It's been part of our ongoing communications discussion,

19:59.410 --> 20:02.250
which included this press conference as one innovation, for example.

20:02.250 --> 20:05.500
There's nothing imminent.

20:05.500 --> 20:10.800
But, again, we'll continue to discuss this, and, as appropriate,

20:10.800 --> 20:13.250
we'll be consulting about it.

20:13.250 --> 20:17.210
BINYAMIN APPELBAUM.

20:17.210 --> 20:19.140
Binya Appelbaum, the New York Times.

20:19.140 --> 20:23.000
What is your assessment of the impact on the United States economy

20:23.000 --> 20:26.630
if there is a default by one or more European nations?

20:26.630 --> 20:29.660
What steps has the Fed taken to assess the consequences

20:29.660 --> 20:31.860
for American financial institutions?

20:31.860 --> 20:36.340
And, in particular, have you examined the impact on derivatives holdings outside

20:36.340 --> 20:38.080
of the regulated financial system?

20:38.080 --> 20:39.590
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

20:39.590 --> 20:43.950
Well, to answer your second question first,

20:43.950 --> 20:49.260
we have been very assiduous in examining the exposures of financial institutions

20:49.260 --> 20:52.470
to the so-called peripheral countries.

20:52.470 --> 20:55.700
The answer is that the banks

20:55.700 --> 21:03.400
that we regulate are not significantly exposed to-to those countries directly,

21:03.400 --> 21:04.580
at least.

21:04.580 --> 21:08.070
They have significant exposures to European banks

21:08.070 --> 21:11.960
in the nonperipheral countries, and so indirectly, they have that exposure.

21:11.960 --> 21:16.950
The-and that statement which I just made includes credit default swaps

21:16.950 --> 21:17.400
and so on.

21:17.400 --> 21:22.890
The gross numbers that the BIS publishes do not fully account for a wide variety

21:22.890 --> 21:24.960
of hedges and other positions.

21:24.960 --> 21:30.230
So we have asked the banks to essentially do stress tests and ask,

21:30.230 --> 21:32.700
looking at all their positions, all their hedges,

21:32.700 --> 21:38.440
What would the effect on their capital be if Greece defaulted?

21:38.440 --> 21:41.740
And the answer is that the effects are very small.

21:41.740 --> 21:50.070
It's also the case that-well, we don't oversee the money market mutual funds.

21:50.070 --> 21:54.430
We have been keeping a close eye on that situation.

21:54.430 --> 21:59.420
There again, the situation is similar in some sense,

21:59.420 --> 22:02.370
in that except-with very few exceptions,

22:02.370 --> 22:06.460
the money market mutual funds don't have much direct exposure

22:06.460 --> 22:08.400
to the three peripheral countries

22:08.400 --> 22:11.870
which are currently dealing with debt problems.

22:11.870 --> 22:15.770
They do have very substantial exposure to European banks

22:15.770 --> 22:18.920
in the so-called core countries: Germany, France, et cetera.

22:18.920 --> 22:26.500
So to the extent that there is indirect impact on-on the core European banks,

22:26.500 --> 22:30.550
that does pose some concern to money market mutual funds

22:30.550 --> 22:35.550
and is a reason why the Federal Reserve and other regulators are continuing

22:35.550 --> 22:39.630
to look at ways to strengthen money market mutual funds.

22:39.630 --> 22:46.980
In terms of the impact of the problem in Greece on the United States,

22:46.980 --> 22:51.980
as I've indicated, the direct exposures are pretty small,

22:51.980 --> 22:56.660
and we're doing all we can to monitor those exposures.

22:56.660 --> 23:02.450
However, as we saw in a small situation, a small case last spring,

23:02.450 --> 23:10.620
a disorderly default in one of those countries would no doubt roil financial

23:10.620 --> 23:11.520
markets globally.

23:11.520 --> 23:17.720
It would have a big impact on credit spreads, on stock prices, and so on.

23:17.720 --> 23:20.180
And so, in that respect, I think the effects

23:20.180 --> 23:22.630
on the United States would be quite significant.

23:22.630 --> 23:25.330
DONNA BORAK.

23:25.330 --> 23:28.290
Mr. Chairman, Donna Borak with American Banker.

23:28.290 --> 23:31.690
To talk about-further about banks, as you know,

23:31.690 --> 23:36.020
global regulators are meeting this week to finalize a proposal

23:36.020 --> 23:41.670
that would name the world's most significantly banks and as well as a surcharge.

23:41.670 --> 23:46.100
There are some that are arguing that regulators are going too far, too fast.

23:46.100 --> 23:49.850
And I'm wondering, for regulators, you know,

23:49.850 --> 23:53.950
where is the line where you-to pass, where you go too far,

23:53.950 --> 23:59.420
and you do hurt credit lending and inevitably hurt the economy?

23:59.420 --> 24:00.420
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

24:00.420 --> 24:01.600
So I'll be attending that meeting,

24:01.600 --> 24:04.050
and so I'll have a chance to hear others' views

24:04.050 --> 24:08.120
and to contribute to that discussion.

24:08.120 --> 24:12.580
It's only been two years since we had the worst financial crisis certainly

24:12.580 --> 24:17.520
since the Great Depression and possibly in the history of the United States.

24:17.520 --> 24:19.720
And the failure or near failure

24:19.720 --> 24:26.100
of large financial institutions was a major contributor to that crisis.

24:26.100 --> 24:30.000
Since we can't know exactly what threats will come in the future,

24:30.000 --> 24:35.350
probably the best all-purpose way of strengthening the balance sheets of banks

24:35.350 --> 24:37.830
and other financial institutions is by capital.

24:37.830 --> 24:44.930
And I'm very supportive of increased capital to-and better quality capital-to

24:44.930 --> 24:51.090
help ensure that these banks will be stable and able to lend in the event

24:51.090 --> 24:53.860
of another crisis, which I hope we don't, of course, ever see.

24:53.860 --> 24:58.910
In terms of the SIFI surcharge, so-called,

24:58.910 --> 25:03.470
I think it's also appropriate to have additional capital requirements

25:03.470 --> 25:07.240
for the largest and most systemically important institutions.

25:07.240 --> 25:13.630
After all, it's because their failure would have very deleterious effects

25:13.630 --> 25:16.710
in the financial system, we need to take extra steps to make sure

25:16.710 --> 25:19.990
that they will be very unlikely to fail.

25:19.990 --> 25:28.780
In addition, it provides some more level playing field because,

25:28.780 --> 25:35.490
by having higher equity requirements, the largest institutions avoid some

25:35.490 --> 25:40.430
of the funding advantages that would otherwise accrue to firms

25:40.430 --> 25:42.650
that are viewed as being too big to fail.

25:42.650 --> 25:46.840
So I think it's very important to do that.

25:46.840 --> 25:49.200
And we'll be negotiating, discussing

25:49.200 --> 25:53.480
with our colleagues internationally what the appropriate number of firms is,

25:53.480 --> 26:01.180
what the appropriate criteria are, and what the amount of capital should be.

26:01.180 --> 26:07.150
In choosing the amount of capital, we will certainly be trying to weigh off

26:07.150 --> 26:08.860
and balance, on the one hand,

26:08.860 --> 26:13.030
the need for extra safety of systemically important firms

26:13.030 --> 26:16.520
against the impacts on lending and so on.

26:16.520 --> 26:20.020
Although I would note that, since systemically important firms are only part

26:20.020 --> 26:23.330
of the banking system, to the extent that they reduce their lending,

26:23.330 --> 26:28.000
some of that lending might go to other institutions.

26:28.000 --> 26:32.500
In terms of going too far, you know, it's very,

26:32.500 --> 26:36.970
very difficult to make a broad-based assessment of the overall impact of all

26:36.970 --> 26:39.020
of the rules and regulations.

26:39.020 --> 26:43.370
But I would like to make clear that, both by law and by our internal practices,

26:43.370 --> 26:48.280
the Federal Reserve does cost-benefit analysis of every rule

26:48.280 --> 26:50.540
that we write, and we publish those.

26:50.540 --> 26:55.470
So we are looking at the cost-benefit for these regulations.

26:55.470 --> 26:59.570
Moreover, we have worked with-the Federal Reserve has worked actively

26:59.570 --> 27:01.860
with the BIS, the Basel Committee,

27:01.860 --> 27:09.540
to do analyses of the effects of capital requirements, on the one hand,

27:09.540 --> 27:12.650
on the probability of a crisis, and on the other hand,

27:12.650 --> 27:15.730
on the cost of lending and the effect on growth.

27:15.730 --> 27:17.880
Those studies have been published.

27:17.880 --> 27:23.780
And if you look at them, you'll see that we believe that the capital

27:23.780 --> 27:28.900
which has been imposed so far would significantly reduce the threat

27:28.900 --> 27:31.840
of a massive financial crisis and, on the other hand,

27:31.840 --> 27:34.000
have very small effects on growth.

27:34.000 --> 27:37.550
So I don't think we're on the wrong side of that tradeoff at this point.

27:37.550 --> 27:39.150
ROBIN HARDING.

27:39.150 --> 27:41.700
Robin Harding from the Financial Times.

27:41.700 --> 27:45.330
Mr. Chairman, you now expect that both headline

27:45.330 --> 27:49.050
and core inflation would be close to your long-run objective in 2012

27:49.050 --> 27:52.870
and 2013 while unemployment remains high.

27:52.870 --> 27:55.150
Does that mean you think the medium-term tradeoff

27:55.150 --> 27:59.200
between inflation and growth has got worse?

27:59.200 --> 28:02.290
And, furthermore, can I ask-Has the unexpected rise

28:02.290 --> 28:05.860
in core inflation changed your understanding of the output gap?

28:05.860 --> 28:06.940
Thank you.

28:06.940 --> 28:09.130
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

28:09.130 --> 28:16.100
Well, to address the latter question first, that's a possibility.

28:16.100 --> 28:21.740
As you saw from the-but as you saw from the projections we just put out,

28:21.740 --> 28:27.290
the Committee-every member of the Committee sees the long-run unemployment

28:27.290 --> 28:30.470
rate-the NAIRU, as it's called by economists-somewhere

28:30.470 --> 28:32.730
around 5½ percent, basically.

28:32.730 --> 28:36.270
So that would suggest that the Committee still believes

28:36.270 --> 28:39.620
that the output gap is quite large.

28:39.620 --> 28:44.560
With respect to core inflation, some of the effects,

28:44.560 --> 28:47.390
at least there, are also temporary.

28:47.390 --> 28:52.450
To name two examples, the supply chain disruptions brought

28:52.450 --> 28:56.780
about by the Japanese disaster have led to a very significant increase

28:56.780 --> 29:02.160
in auto prices, both new and used automobiles, last month.

29:02.160 --> 29:06.920
As these problems are resolved-and they appear to be very much on the way

29:06.920 --> 29:12.330
to being resolved-we would assume that the auto prices would come back down

29:12.330 --> 29:18.930
and incentives would be restored as competition increases and costs are reduced.

29:18.930 --> 29:20.950
So that's one example.

29:20.950 --> 29:24.890
Another would be the fact that energy prices have passed

29:24.890 --> 29:29.300
through to a number of-despite the fact that core is defined excluding energy,

29:29.300 --> 29:34.970
that's only the energy, direct energy products-things like airfares,

29:34.970 --> 29:39.030
for example, which are very sensitive to the cost of jet fuel,

29:39.030 --> 29:41.190
are also a part of the core.

29:41.190 --> 29:45.250
So you would imagine that, as the price of oil declines,

29:45.250 --> 29:48.470
that you would see some bounce-decline in,

29:48.470 --> 29:54.600
also in-in the core measures of inflation.

29:54.600 --> 29:59.050
So given that there's still a large output gap,

29:59.050 --> 30:04.720
given that inflation expectations remain well anchored,

30:04.720 --> 30:08.540
given that some of the temporary factors affecting inflation,

30:08.540 --> 30:13.010
including core inflation, are likely to recede, I think it's reasonable to think

30:13.010 --> 30:18.790
that core inflation will fall back towards mandate-consistent levels.

30:18.790 --> 30:21.100
That being said, I think it's the case,

30:21.100 --> 30:25.180
if you look at the projections that we have marked up a little bit,

30:25.180 --> 30:29.720
the near-term and core projections.

30:29.720 --> 30:30.870
PETER BARNES.

30:30.870 --> 30:33.310
Peter Barnes, Fox Business, Mr. Chairman.

30:33.310 --> 30:37.140
Mr. Chairman, what is the extended period right now

30:37.140 --> 30:41.250
for exceptionally low fed funds rate-rates,

30:41.250 --> 30:45.420
given the recent developments in the U.S. and global economic picture?

30:45.420 --> 30:47.510
Is it a year or two?

30:47.510 --> 30:52.690
And under what conditions would the extended period be extended even longer?

30:52.690 --> 30:53.290
Thank you.

30:53.290 --> 30:53.800
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

30:53.800 --> 30:56.650
Well, you know, the reason we use terms like "extended period" is not

30:56.650 --> 30:59.810
to be intentionally opaque.

30:59.810 --> 31:02.580
The reason is that we don't know exactly how long.

31:02.580 --> 31:08.580
I think the-I think the thrust of "extended period" is that we believe we're

31:08.580 --> 31:13.560
at least two or three meetings away from taking any further action,

31:13.560 --> 31:15.500
and I emphasize "at least."

31:15.500 --> 31:22.450
But depending on how the economy evolves, and inflation and unemployment,

31:22.450 --> 31:25.350
it could be, you know, significantly longer.

31:25.350 --> 31:30.440
It will depend on how the economy and the economic outlook changes.

31:30.440 --> 31:39.580
If we do get both improved job creation and inflation close to our-close to

31:39.580 --> 31:41.570
or even above our mandate-consistent level,

31:41.570 --> 31:45.590
then that would be a sign that we need to consider beginning an exit process.

31:45.590 --> 31:48.220
But we're not there at this point.

31:48.220 --> 31:51.700
PETER BARNES.

31:51.700 --> 31:56.180
But what about negative conditions-for example,

31:56.180 --> 32:00.950
the situation in Europe becoming a contagion situation?

32:00.950 --> 32:02.000
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

32:02.000 --> 32:07.770
Well, if the economy worsens and inflation remains relatively low,

32:07.770 --> 32:11.200
then we wouldn't begin to exit, and, therefore, we wouldn't change the language.

32:11.200 --> 32:17.480
The expected length of keeping rates low would then be longer, but, again,

32:17.480 --> 32:20.730
we wouldn't want to give explicit-we could, I suppose,

32:20.730 --> 32:25.780
but we have at least not chosen so far to give an explicit time frame,

32:25.780 --> 32:30.540
again because it's our intention to continue to monitor the economy,

32:30.540 --> 32:33.310
revise our outlook-we just revised our outlook fairly significantly

32:33.310 --> 32:38.670
since April-and make a judgment based on the incoming data.

32:38.670 --> 32:45.410
So we don't want to commit ourselves necessarily to a fixed-a fixed time frame.

32:45.410 --> 32:48.840
CRAIG TORRES.

32:48.840 --> 32:51.950
Mr. Chairman, Craig Torres from Bloomberg News.

32:51.950 --> 32:52.730
Cool fan charts.

32:52.730 --> 32:53.420
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

32:53.420 --> 32:53.790
Thank you.

32:53.790 --> 32:54.800
CRAIG TORRES.

32:54.800 --> 33:01.210
So, all of you have to make policy projections on appropriate policy.

33:01.210 --> 33:05.510
So to get to this world of 2013, when we have above-trend growth

33:05.510 --> 33:09.390
and unemployment falling and inflation at a really nice level,

33:09.390 --> 33:12.420
what would your appropriate policy be to get there?

33:12.420 --> 33:18.210
Would it be an early gradual exit, a late steep exit, or no exit?

33:18.210 --> 33:20.200
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

33:20.200 --> 33:22.260
Well, I don't think it would be constructive for me

33:22.260 --> 33:30.060
to give you a tentative projection because, obviously, as I've indicated,

33:30.060 --> 33:34.120
there's simply no alternative but to watch the incoming data

33:34.120 --> 33:38.920
and to make judgments both in terms of when the exit should begin, which,

33:38.920 --> 33:43.960
at this point, is going to depend on incoming data,

33:43.960 --> 33:50.920
but also what the slope of tightening would be, how quickly we would tighten.

33:50.920 --> 33:57.260
So, you know, we all have estimates in our minds, but, you know,

33:57.260 --> 34:01.530
that's a very far cry from saying that this is what's going to happen.

34:01.530 --> 34:05.310
In a sense, the FOMC has to forecast its own behavior

34:05.310 --> 34:08.030
in the same way it forecasts the economy.

34:08.030 --> 34:10.220
And that forecast can change.

34:10.220 --> 34:14.250
And given developments in the economy, we might end up doing something different

34:14.250 --> 34:18.490
than what we currently think is most likely.

34:18.490 --> 34:20.090
KEVIN HALL.

34:20.090 --> 34:24.840
Kevin Hall of McClatchy Newspapers.

34:24.840 --> 34:27.730
If I understand correctly, unlike your predecessor,

34:27.730 --> 34:32.140
you're actually bringing your own forecast to the FOMC meeting.

34:32.140 --> 34:36.100
As a first among equals, how did you fall in the central tendency?

34:36.100 --> 34:39.360
Were you among the three that were excluded on the high end or the low end?

34:39.360 --> 34:42.510
And on the range-where would you put yourself on the range

34:42.510 --> 34:45.460
of the forecasts as a first among equals?

34:45.460 --> 34:49.000
And as a side note, I don't think your predecessor did this.

34:49.000 --> 34:53.600
Why did-what benefits do you get by bringing your own forecast?

34:53.600 --> 34:56.680
And the-your predecessor made headlines recently,

34:56.680 --> 35:00.300
suggesting that we should urgently move back to the Clinton-era tax levels

35:00.300 --> 35:03.300
because of how bad the debt situation could get.

35:03.300 --> 35:07.150
Without getting into the area of Congress, what they should or shouldn't do,

35:07.150 --> 35:10.870
what would be the benefits or costs of moving back

35:10.870 --> 35:15.310
to a 1999 3 percentage point change in the marginal tax rates?

35:15.310 --> 35:15.890
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

35:15.890 --> 35:18.830
Well, in your first question, I'm a member of the FOMC,

35:18.830 --> 35:24.640
and so I have submitted my own forecasts.

35:24.640 --> 35:30.800
I think I would characterize me as-myself as being pretty consistent

35:30.800 --> 35:31.950
with most of my colleagues.

35:31.950 --> 35:40.190
I certainly am not taking an outsized extreme view in any way.

35:40.190 --> 35:45.440
And, in particular, I do personally believe that the slowdown is

35:45.440 --> 35:52.070
at least partly temporary, and that we'll see greater growth going forward.

35:52.070 --> 35:56.830
At the same time, I did-I do think that,

35:56.830 --> 35:59.410
given that we can't explain the entire slowdown,

35:59.410 --> 36:03.440
that the best guess would be that growth, at least in the near term,

36:03.440 --> 36:07.070
might be a little bit less than we anticipated.

36:07.070 --> 36:12.910
On tax policy, you know, I think the main point I'd like to make-and

36:12.910 --> 36:17.450
as you know, I'm reluctant to get into specifics of tax

36:17.450 --> 36:20.660
and spending policy-the main point I'd like to make is

36:20.660 --> 36:22.300
that we do need very seriously

36:22.300 --> 36:28.290
and urgently to address the overall fiscal situation,

36:28.290 --> 36:32.840
in particular by taking a long-run perspective to do that.

36:32.840 --> 36:37.100
Exactly how that's done is really the responsibility of Congress

36:37.100 --> 36:40.930
because there are different tradeoffs from different choices.

36:40.930 --> 36:44.840
And, of course, those choices also reflect fundamental values

36:44.840 --> 36:48.160
in what-about what you think the government should do,

36:48.160 --> 36:53.060
how much resources the government should command through taxes, and so on.

36:53.060 --> 36:56.790
So I don't generally make recommendations about specifics.

36:56.790 --> 37:01.790
But I do think, in my role as someone who's extremely interested

37:01.790 --> 37:05.870
in financial stability, that addressing the medium-

37:05.870 --> 37:08.540
to long-term deficit problem is very urgent.

37:08.540 --> 37:10.980
MICHELLE FLEURY.

37:10.980 --> 37:13.160
Michelle Fleury, BBC News.

37:13.160 --> 37:15.040
Looking at your unemployment projections,

37:15.040 --> 37:20.710
it seems you expect weak growth-weak jobs growth going forward and then a return

37:20.710 --> 37:22.730
to normal, sort of, in the long run.

37:22.730 --> 37:26.060
Does this mean, then, you don't expect that there's a structural issue here?

37:26.060 --> 37:29.000
And, if not, can you sort of give us a time frame for sort

37:29.000 --> 37:31.450
of in the long run, when we return to normal?

37:31.450 --> 37:32.340
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

37:32.340 --> 37:37.940
Well, we expect-as the projections show, we expect growth in the second half

37:37.940 --> 37:43.170
of this year and next year to be certainly faster than it's been so far in 2011.

37:43.170 --> 37:48.820
And as a result, we would expect to see healthier job creation numbers.

37:48.820 --> 37:51.500
So we should see-if our forecast is correct,

37:51.500 --> 37:55.060
we'll see payroll numbers improving relatively soon.

37:55.060 --> 38:01.500
In terms of the unemployment rate, though,

38:01.500 --> 38:05.900
given that growth is not much above the long-run potential rate

38:05.900 --> 38:10.720
of growth-and we have in our projections an estimate of 2.5 to 2.8 percent,

38:10.720 --> 38:16.300
we haven't really done much better than that-it takes growth faster

38:16.300 --> 38:18.500
than potential to bring down unemployment.

38:18.500 --> 38:22.650
And since we're not getting that, we project unemployment to come

38:22.650 --> 38:25.370
down very painfully slowly.

38:25.370 --> 38:31.420
At some point, if growth picks up as we anticipate,

38:31.420 --> 38:33.830
job numbers will start getting better.

38:33.830 --> 38:39.560
We're still some years away from full employment in the sense of 5½ percent,

38:39.560 --> 38:44.850
say, and that's, of course, very frustrating because it means

38:44.850 --> 38:49.500
that many people will be out of work for a very extended time.

38:49.500 --> 38:55.120
And that can have significant long-term consequences that concern me very much.

38:55.120 --> 38:58.260
MARK FELSENTHAL.

38:58.260 --> 38:59.860
Mark Felsenthal, with Reuters.

38:59.860 --> 39:02.890
Mr. Chairman, given your response just now,

39:02.890 --> 39:09.150
and given the Fed's belief that the recent uptick inflation is transitory,

39:09.150 --> 39:13.180
why wouldn't the Fed consider taking more action to stimulate growth?

39:13.180 --> 39:16.780
And if it would consider that, would bond purchases be your preference,

39:16.780 --> 39:21.640
or would communications tactics, such as the one suggested by Jon,

39:21.640 --> 39:25.380
also be on the table as you consider how to get the economy growing again?

39:25.380 --> 39:27.200
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

39:27.200 --> 39:28.630
Well, there are a couple of considerations.

39:28.630 --> 39:31.050
One, as I indicated before, is

39:31.050 --> 39:36.140
that the current outlook is significantly different

39:36.140 --> 39:42.040
than what we were facing in-in August of last year.

39:42.040 --> 39:43.870
We no longer have a deflation risk.

39:43.870 --> 39:47.250
Inflation is above-at the moment, is above target.

39:47.250 --> 39:52.000
We expect it to fall, but we're no longer-certainly not

39:52.000 --> 39:55.830
in any deflationary situation.

39:55.830 --> 39:58.630
And notwithstanding the disappointing news recently,

39:58.630 --> 40:04.010
the labor market has been performing better than it was last year.

40:04.010 --> 40:10.890
On top of that, we have an awful lot of uncertainty right now about how much

40:10.890 --> 40:14.010
of this slowdown is temporary, how much is permanent.

40:14.010 --> 40:16.820
So that would suggest, all else equal,

40:16.820 --> 40:20.970
that a little bit of time to see what's going to happen is-it would be useful

40:20.970 --> 40:23.100
in making policy decisions.

40:23.100 --> 40:26.600
We'll continue to look at the outlook and act, you know,

40:26.600 --> 40:32.030
as appropriately as the news comes in and the projections change.

40:32.030 --> 40:38.420
We do have a number of ways of acting; none of them are without risks or costs.

40:38.420 --> 40:41.130
We could, for example, do more securities purchases

40:41.130 --> 40:43.090
and structure them in different ways.

40:43.090 --> 40:48.430
We could cut the interest on excess reserves that we pay to banks.

40:48.430 --> 40:53.170
And as was suggested by an earlier question-several earlier questions,

40:53.170 --> 40:57.220
actually-Jon's question about giving guidance on the balance sheet

40:57.220 --> 41:05.150
or by perhaps even giving a fixed date, you know, to define "extended period,"

41:05.150 --> 41:07.980
those are ways that we could ease further if needed.

41:07.980 --> 41:11.100
But, of course, all of these things are somewhat untested.

41:11.100 --> 41:14.530
They have their own costs.

41:14.530 --> 41:18.070
But we'd be prepared to take additional action,

41:18.070 --> 41:21.200
obviously, if conditions warranted.

41:21.200 --> 41:22.720
AKIHIRO OKADA.

41:22.720 --> 41:29.600
Mr. Chairman, I am Akihiro Okada with Yomiuri Shimbun, a Japanese newspaper.

41:29.600 --> 41:33.280
During the Japanese lost decade in the 1990s,

41:33.280 --> 41:36.840
you strongly criticized Japan's lack of policies.

41:36.840 --> 41:41.410
Recently Larry Summers suggested in his column that the U.S. is

41:41.410 --> 41:44.370
in the middle of its own lost decade.

41:44.370 --> 41:50.580
Based on those points with QE2 ending, what do you think of Japan's experience

41:50.580 --> 41:53.440
and the reality facing the U.S.?

41:53.440 --> 41:58.840
Are there any historical lessons that we should be reminded about?

41:58.840 --> 42:00.280
Thank you.

42:00.280 --> 42:02.410
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

42:02.410 --> 42:03.920
Well, I'm a little bit more sympathetic

42:03.920 --> 42:08.080
to central bankers now than I was 10 years ago.

42:08.080 --> 42:16.740
I think it's very important to understand that in my comments-both in my comment

42:16.740 --> 42:21.620
in the published comment a decade ago as well as in my speech in 2002

42:21.620 --> 42:25.790
about deflation-my main point was

42:25.790 --> 42:32.840
that a determined central bank can always do something about deflation.

42:32.840 --> 42:37.170
After all, inflation is a monetary phenomenon,

42:37.170 --> 42:40.030
a central bank can always create money, and so on.

42:40.030 --> 42:45.580
I also argued-and I think it's well understood that deflation,

42:45.580 --> 42:51.420
persistent deflation can be a very debilitating factor in-in growth

42:51.420 --> 42:55.600
and employment in an economy.

42:55.600 --> 43:01.910
So we acted on that advice here in the United States, as I just described,

43:01.910 --> 43:06.680
in August, September of last year.

43:06.680 --> 43:14.340
We could infer from, say, TIPS prices-inflation index bond prices-that investors

43:14.340 --> 43:16.480
saw something on the order of a one-third chance

43:16.480 --> 43:18.670
of outright deflation going forward.

43:18.670 --> 43:21.700
So there was a significant risk there.

43:21.700 --> 43:29.500
The securities purchases that we did were intended, in part,

43:29.500 --> 43:31.930
to end that risk of deflation.

43:31.930 --> 43:37.680
And I think it's widely agreed that we succeeded in ending that deflation risk.

43:37.680 --> 43:41.590
I think also that our policies were constructive on the employment side.

43:41.590 --> 43:44.040
This, I realize, is a bit more controversial.

43:44.040 --> 43:48.950
But we did take actions as needed, even though we were at the zero lower bound

43:48.950 --> 43:51.460
of interest rates, to address deflation.

43:51.460 --> 43:55.770
So that was the thrust of my remarks 10 years ago.

43:55.770 --> 43:59.480
And we've been consistent with that-with that approach.

43:59.480 --> 44:00.880
STEVE BECKNER.

44:00.880 --> 44:04.400
Mr. Chairman, Steve Beckner, Market News International.

44:04.400 --> 44:09.680
Do you and your colleagues have a statistical trigger of any sorts, say,

44:09.680 --> 44:12.320
a particular level of unemployment

44:12.320 --> 44:15.850
or inflation at which you would begin the exit process?

44:15.850 --> 44:18.160
If you do, wouldn't it make sense to announce it?

44:18.160 --> 44:19.460
If not, why not?

44:19.460 --> 44:21.230
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

44:21.230 --> 44:26.150
Well, it's pretty impossible to create a statistical trigger

44:26.150 --> 44:31.660
because we have currently 17 independent members of the FOMC.

44:31.660 --> 44:40.760
Each has his or her own view on the outlook, on the efficacy of monetary policy,

44:40.760 --> 44:45.260
and on the risks to inflation and unemployment.

44:45.260 --> 44:49.450
So we don't have any such formula.

44:49.450 --> 44:54.450
We have staff produce, you know, various scenarios,

44:54.450 --> 44:58.860
which give some indication of-given their projections

44:58.860 --> 45:02.080
of where the most likely outcomes,

45:02.080 --> 45:07.530
most likely points for a beginning of an exit would be.

45:07.530 --> 45:13.190
But as I said earlier when I was asked about my own projections,

45:13.190 --> 45:17.910
those are very tentative, depend on a lot happening,

45:17.910 --> 45:21.550
depend on the forecasts evolving as expected,

45:21.550 --> 45:26.840
and are certainly subject to change as new information comes in.

45:26.840 --> 45:28.990
DEREK KRAVITZ.

45:28.990 --> 45:30.540
Derek Kravitz, Associated Press.

45:30.540 --> 45:37.220
On your point about permanent factors, housing, back in February-actually,

45:37.220 --> 45:43.000
November, you had said that a second asset-a second round

45:43.000 --> 45:46.750
of asset purchases would go ahead and ease mortgage rates

45:46.750 --> 45:49.870
and make housing more affordable.

45:49.870 --> 45:53.670
You've seen housing become more affordable; you've seen rates decline.

45:53.670 --> 45:56.090
But you're seeing underlying fundamentals

45:56.090 --> 45:59.700
in the sector still very weak-starts, sales, and prices.

45:59.700 --> 46:04.990
Many economists have pushed back any and all economic forecasts for the sector

46:04.990 --> 46:08.320
to 2013-2014 for any meaningful rebound.

46:08.320 --> 46:13.210
What could be done for that sector as far as-to stimulate growth?

46:13.210 --> 46:15.500
CHAIRMAN BERNANKE.

46:15.500 --> 46:18.660
Well, the housing sector is very important to the overall recovery,

46:18.660 --> 46:21.780
and so we've paid a lot of attention to that.

46:21.780 --> 46:24.150
We did-as you point out, we did succeed

46:24.150 --> 46:28.820
in significantly lowering the mortgage rates.

46:28.820 --> 46:34.310
So those who can get credit, together with the low prices of houses,

46:34.310 --> 46:39.840
are at-able to buy much more house than they could have a few years ago.

46:39.840 --> 46:44.490
Unfortunately, there are problems, including the fact that credit standards

46:44.490 --> 46:47.720
for mortgages have tightened quite considerably

46:47.720 --> 46:52.670
so that roughly a bottom-the bottom third of people who might have qualified

46:52.670 --> 46:54.390
for a prime mortgage in terms of, say,

46:54.390 --> 47:00.780
FICO scores a few years ago cannot qualify today.

47:00.780 --> 47:03.440
So that's-that's certainly an important problem.

47:03.440 --> 47:09.180
There's also evidently a lot of uncertainty about employment,

47:09.180 --> 47:13.020
about the economic recovery, and that's affecting people's willingness

47:13.020 --> 47:15.330
to make the commitment to buy a house.

47:15.330 --> 47:19.940
So there are a number of fundamental factors

47:19.940 --> 47:23.400
which are slowing the housing market down,

47:23.400 --> 47:26.410
and they do present very difficult challenges.

47:26.410 --> 47:31.180
The Fed is trying to address this in a number of ways.

47:31.180 --> 47:35.800
Of course, our monetary policy is intended to try to promote employment

47:35.800 --> 47:40.040
and income gains, which, of course, will help housing demand.

47:40.040 --> 47:47.050
As regulators, we have recently issued cease-and-desist orders to servicers

47:47.050 --> 47:49.510
to try and improve servicing practices.

47:49.510 --> 47:55.480
We work with our regulated banks to ask them to do modifications

47:55.480 --> 48:00.690
where appropriate and to manage their REO-real estate owned-real estate

48:00.690 --> 48:03.040
in an economy-supportive way.

48:03.040 --> 48:13.750
We've also-the Federal Reserve has also been very much involved in giving input

48:13.750 --> 48:18.480
to other agencies which have responsibilities for housing.

48:18.480 --> 48:22.080
For example, we have provided advice to the Treasury

48:22.080 --> 48:24.030
on their modification programs.

48:24.030 --> 48:31.690
In fact, I am-ex officio, I am the head of the oversight board for the TARP,

48:31.690 --> 48:37.880
which is-now mostly consists of HAMP, the housing program.

48:37.880 --> 48:40.210
So, in that context, I'm kept well informed.

48:40.210 --> 48:44.960
So the Federal Reserve is doing a lot and doing what it can.

48:44.960 --> 48:51.730
I think otherwise I'd like to see just further efforts to-first of all,

48:51.730 --> 48:57.790
to modify loans where appropriate; to-and where not appropriate,

48:57.790 --> 49:05.070
to speed the process of foreclosure and disposition of the foreclosed homes

49:05.070 --> 49:10.880
in order to clear the market; get these homes out of the pipeline;

49:10.880 --> 49:14.320
and allow people to, you know,

49:14.320 --> 49:18.440
to operate in a market where they're more confident

49:18.440 --> 49:21.470
that prices will be stable rather than falling.

49:21.470 --> 49:27.810
It's interesting now that-although house prices overall are declining,

49:27.810 --> 49:31.980
all of that is concentrated in distressed properties; that is,

49:31.980 --> 49:33.790
houses which are not being sold

49:33.790 --> 49:37.320
on a distressed basis have much more stable prices than those

49:37.320 --> 49:39.620
which are being sold on a distressed basis.

49:39.620 --> 49:45.420
And that suggests that, if we can reduce the current number and something

49:45.420 --> 49:48.970
of more than a third, maybe 40 percent of home sales which are

49:48.970 --> 49:53.900
on a distressed basis, that would do a lot to stabilizing the market

49:53.900 --> 49:57.480
and helping give people confidence that they can buy,

49:57.480 --> 50:00.560
and not be buying into a falling market.

50:00.560 --> 50:02.640
Thank you very much.

50:02.640 --> 50:11.960
